  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Tritium isotope of hydrogen is radioactive and emits low energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>&#946;</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math> particles. It is because of&nbsp;high <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> ratio of tritium which makes nucleus unstable.</p><p>For tritium <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mmultiscripts></mfenced></math></p><p>No. of neutron <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p>No. of proton <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8757;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mfrac></math> is high</p><p>tritium wil emit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#946;</mi></math> particle.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
